STORY: {
  idea: "Children hide their inner world not because they distrust their parents but because they love them so much that disappointing them feels terrifying. Seven things they cannot say — listen, don't fix; I notice your phone; your stress becomes mine; stop comparing me; forgive me like you forgive yourself; I'm scared to disappoint you; love me most when I'm hardest to love — all point at one goal: a home where telling the truth feels safe.",
  arc: "We open on a hug at the front door — and THE JAR SON hides behind his back, lid screwed tight. Not drugs, not secrets: thoughts, fears, tiny moments MOM forgot and he kept. Seven jars stand on his shelf; each chapter opens one. MOM learns to sit inside the blanket fort instead of launching an investigation; to look up from THE PHONE before the little boy with the frog stops talking and the teen behind the closed door stops trying; to say the one sentence that lifts the weight of his imagination; to stop measuring him against other marks on the door frame; to repair her own mistakes out loud (the gold-seamed mug); to make failure safe enough to bring home (the bad test pinned next to the good one) — and, in the storm of the slammed door and 'I hate you', to correct, wait and love all at once while his spikes soften.",
  ending: "Cautious closeness — at the kitchen table SON unscrews THE JAR himself and tells MOM the truth while her phone lies face down; the front-door hug returns with nothing behind his back. Relief, not a perfect happy ending.",
},

PLAN: [
  { sequence: "00 What they hide", purpose: "Hook: love and hiding in one hug — THE JAR behind SON's back. Not the dangers parents fear, but small things kept since much earlier; children hide out of love, not distrust. Set up the seven jars on his shelf and tease number six.", feel: "Warmth with a hidden edge, intrigue, then tender worry.", mood: "EVENING", metaphor: "the jar behind his back; giant crossed-out fears; the moment kept in a jar; walking on thin ice toward MOM; the seven jars", open: true, payoff: "S232" },
  { sequence: "01 Listen, not fix", purpose: "Number one: when SON says today was terrible, MOM's brain launches a federal investigation; what he wanted was someone to sit inside the feeling with him — the blanket fort. DAD's whiteboard shows adults want the same; then MOM and SON carry the jar together.", feel: "Comic overreaction turning into tenderness.", mood: "ACCENT", metaphor: "MOM repairing SON like a machine; the alarm on her bun; the kitchen as detective HQ; the blanket fort; DAD's whiteboard flying out of the window; carrying the jar together", open: false, payoff: "" },
  { sequence: "02 The phone", purpose: "Number two: THE PHONE pulls MOM in while LITTLE BOY tugs; she has good reasons, but he only sees her eyes; his story shrinks to nothing; the door closes a little more each year until the teen won't try — so the frog in the jar today is practice for the secret at fifteen.", feel: "A quiet ache that grows over years.", mood: "NEUTRAL", metaphor: "the giant phone swallowing MOM; the story bubble shrinking; the closing door across the years; the frog jar becoming the secret jar", open: false, payoff: "" },
  { sequence: "03 Your stress", purpose: "Number three: SON is an emotional detective — the silence, the bill, the whispers, DAD on the couch, MOM's smile mask; when he can't understand, his crayon fills the gap with worse; one honest sentence lifts the giant jar off his back.", feel: "Creeping anxiety spiralling to a peak, then relief.", mood: "COOL", metaphor: "the knot that jumps from MOM to SON; the child's clue board; the crayon scribble filling the gap; the escalating thought bubbles; the giant jar lifted; the seesaw where one small sentence outweighs a boulder", open: false, payoff: "" },
  { sequence: "04 Stop comparing me", purpose: "Number four: the pencil marks on the kitchen door frame measure SON against his brother, his sister and the impossible COUSIN KEVIN; the boss and SARAH show MOM how it feels; SON's real need is standards and a place he doesn't compete for — he steps out of the dotted outline into his own mark.", feel: "A sting, a big laugh, empathy, then warm relief.", mood: "ACCENT", metaphor: "the height marks on the door frame; KEVIN's winners' podium; the star bait on a fishing rod; MOM squeezed into SARAH's outline; the evidence bag; SON's own chair; the dotted outline erased", open: false, payoff: "" },
  { sequence: "05 Forgive me too", purpose: "Number five: MOM the judge dismisses her own mistakes and reads a long scroll at SON's; DAD's oops vs SON's homework, MOM's keys vs SON's bottle; adults land on a cushion, children on a red circle; the answer is not perfection but recovery — the gold-seamed mug.", feel: "Comic recognition turning into warm repair.", mood: "ACCENT", metaphor: "the judge's gavel and the long scroll; the megaphone; the keys in MOM's bun; the cushion vs the red pen; MOM stepping off the perfect pedestal; the gold-seamed mug and the mended jar", open: false, payoff: "" },
  { sequence: "06 Scared to disappoint you", purpose: "Number six (the tease from S18): under the grade lies the fear of MOM's face; SON checks the emotional weather before showing anything; when love seems brightest after achievement, the spotlight dims and things get hidden — so make failure safe to bring home, because one day the mistake will be much bigger.", feel: "Held breath and deep fear, a warm fix, then a stark choice in the night.", mood: "NEUTRAL", metaphor: "the iceberg with MOM's frown below; the frozen spill; the umbrella weather check; the spotlight that dims; the jars hidden under the bed; SON's own smile mask; the bad test pinned beside the good one; the jar buried at night", open: false, payoff: "" },
  { sequence: "07 Hardest to love", purpose: "Number seven: the slammed door, the eye roll, the tantrum, 'I hate you' — boundaries still matter, but under the ugliest moments is a quiet question: what happens to us when I'm at my worst? MOM waits on the other side of the door, corrects, waits and loves all at once, and his spikes soften.", feel: "Storm, hurt, then sturdy calm love.", mood: "TENSE", metaphor: "SON's hair turned to hedgehog spikes and MOM's hug anyway; the little boy inside the angry teen's thought; back to back through the door; MOM juggling the stop paddle, the sand timer and the heart", open: false, payoff: "" },
  { sequence: "08 Safe to tell", purpose: "Ending: children don't need perfect parents but enough moments that say I see you, I hear you, you can make mistakes here — the toys and holidays lose their colour, but the safety of telling the truth stays: SON opens THE JAR at the kitchen table and the front-door hug returns with nothing hidden.", feel: "Tender recognition settling into quiet relief.", mood: "DUSK", metaphor: "the open jar filling with gold moments; callbacks to the fort, the mended mug, the door; forgotten things losing their colour; the jar opened at last", open: false, payoff: "" },
],

